The Clock System controls how fast the STM32 CPU and peripherals operate. Think of it as the
heartbeat of the microcontroller.

For example: the CPU clock determines how quickly instructions execute, while peripheral clocks
control modules such as GPIO, UART, SPI, and timers.


A. CPU Clock
	Instruction Sample:
	
	int a = 10;
	int b = 20;
	int c = a + b;
	
	The compiler converts it into machine instructions that the CPU can execute. 
	
	Instruction (Conceptual) 			Meaning
	LOAD								Load a value into a CPU Register
	ADD									Add two values
	STORE								Save a result to memory
	
	Note: The CPU clock determines how quickly the processor can execute these instructions, though
	one instruction may take multiple clock cycles.

B. Peripherals Clock
	Imagine you want to turn ON an LED using a microcontroller.
	
	GPIOA->ODR |= (1U << 5); // PA5: index 5 to 1 (HIGH)
	
	Two things must happen:
	a. CPU Clock: The CPU executes instructions to read, modify, and write the GPIO register.
	b. GPIO Peripheral Clock: The GPIO hardware/ports must receive its clock if the microcontorller
	   requires it to be enabled. Otherwise, GPIO register access or operation may not work as
	   expected. Basically, it drives the timing or operation of hardware modules. 
	   
	Why do we enable/disable the GPIO clock?
		- Enable GPIO clock: Allows the microcontroller to access and operate the GPIO peripheral.
		- Disable GPIO clock: Reduces power consumption when the GPIO peripheral is no longer needed, on MCUs that support clock gating.
		- CPU processing: The CPU still executes instructions to configure and control GPIO. Enabling the GPIO clock does not automatically
		  reduce CPU workload.

1. Clock Sources
a. HSI - High Speed Internal: Application Startup
b. HSE - High Speed External: To configure a higher-performance clock
c. LSI - Low Speed Internal: Independent Watchdog (IWDG)
d. LSE - Low Speed External: Real-Time Clock (RTC) to maintain time.

2. PLL - Phase-Locked Loop
   The PLL lets us generate a high-frequency system clock from a lower-frequency input.
   
   SYSCLK = (HSE / M ) * (N / P)
   
   Important: PLL settings must also satisfy the MCU's input-frequency, VCO-Frequency, and 
   output-frequency limits.

3. Clock Tree and Buses
   a. SYSCLK - 168 MHz
   b. AHB - 168 MHz (CPU, Memory, DMA Bus Domains)
   c. APB1 - 42 MHz (USART2, I2C, TIM2)
   d. APB2 - 84 MHz (USART1, SPI1, TIM1)


	   
